mpute distance using squared Euclidean without sqrt.\n\nHeap implementation:\nWe'll maintain two arrays of size k: heap_dist (max-heap) and heap_label. Use 0-indexed array with sift-down on replacement.\n\nPseudo:\n\nvoid heap_push(float dist, char label, float *heap_dist, char *heap_label, int *size) {\n    if (*size < k) {\n        // insert at end\n        int i = (*size)++;\n        heap_dist[i] = dist;\n        heap_label[i] = label;\n        // sift up (but we want max-heap: parent larger than children)\n        while (i > 0 && heap_dist[(i-1)/2] < heap_dist[i]) {\n            swap with parent\n        }\n    } else if (dist < heap_dist[0]) { // new distance smaller than current max in heap\n        heap_dist[0] = dist;\n        heap_label[0] = label;\n        // sift down\n        int i=0;\n        while (1) {\n            int largest = i;\n            int left = 2*i+1, right = 2*i+2;\n            if (left < k && heap_dist[left] > heap_dist[largest]) largest = left;\n            if (right < k && heap_dist[right] > heap_dist[largest]) largest = right;\n            if (largest != i) {\n                swap\n                i = largest;\n            } else break;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nBut we need to initialize heap with -1 distances? Actually, initial distances are -1 meaning empty. We can treat that as infinite distance for max-heap: when size < k, just insert; after full, compare.\n\nSimplify: Use a fixed-size array and maintain count. Initially fill with INF (like FLT_MAX) so that any real distance is smaller. Then we always do sift-down on root if new dist < root? Actually for top-k smallest, we want to keep the k smallest distances in a max-heap where root is largest among them. So when heap is full and new dist < root, replace root and sift down.\n\nImplementation: initialize all distances to INF (e.g., 1e30f). Then for each point, if dist < heap_dist[0], then replace and sift-down. This avoids separate size tracking.\n\nWe'll use FLT_MAX from float.h.\n\nAlso, after processing all points, we need to extract labels from the heap? Actually we just need the most frequent label among top-k distances. The heap contains the k smallest distances (with their labels). But they are not sorted; we can sort them using qsort on labels array (size k) as before. That's fine.\n\nSo algorithm:\n\n- Allocate arrays dists[k] and labels[k], initialize dists to FLT_MAX.\n- For each group, for each point:\n    float d = euclidean_distance_no_sqrt(to_evaluate.x - xs[j], ...);\n    if (d < dists[0]) {\n        // replace root\n        dists[0] = d;\n        labels[0] = g.label;\n        // sift down\n        int i=0, largest;\n        while ((largest = 2*i+1) < k) { // left child exists\n            if (largest+1 < k && dists[largest+1] > dists[largest]) largest++;\n            if (dists[i] >= dists[largest]) break;\n            swap(dists[i], dists[largest]);\n            swap(labels[i], labels[largest]);\n            i = largest;\n        }\n    }\n\nAfter loop, sort labels with qsort and find most frequent.\n\nWe also need to modify euclidean_distance_no_sqrt to take two floats? Actually we can compute directly: (dx*dx + dy*dy). We'll keep the function but change signature or inline.\n\nTo enable SIMD auto-vectorization of distance computation, we should write a loop that computes distances and then updates heap. But the update is conditional; compiler may not vectorize across points because of dependency on heap state. However, if we compute all distances first into an array (size N) and then process them sequentially? That would require O(N) memory but could allow SIMD for distance computation. Then after that, sequential heap updates. This might be beneficial if N is large.\n\nGiven the previous SoA+NEON version likely computed 4 at a time with intrinsics, this approach may not beat it. But we can try.\n\nLet's decide: We'll compute distances on-the-fly but use OpenMP SIMD pragma to hint vectorization of the inner loop over points?